{"title":"Email overload: exploring personal information management of email","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/238386.238530","doi":"10.1145/238386.238530","authors":["Steve Whittaker","Candace L. Sidner"],"year":1996,"published":"1996-04-13","abstract":"An empirical study of email use showing that inboxes serve not only communication but also task management and archiving functions. Outstanding tasks and ongoing conversational threads coexist with other messages, creating problems of status tracking and overlooked important information.","venue":"CHI '96"}
{"title":"Supporting Prospective Information in Email","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/634067.634150","doi":"10.1145/634067.634150","authors":["Jacek Gwizdka"],"year":2001,"abstract":"Studies email as a repository for prospective information about future actions and events. It links observed email-management behavior to prospective-memory requirements and identifies limitations in conventional email interfaces for managing time-sensitive future work."}
{"title":"Email Task Management Styles: The Cleaners and the Keepers","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/985921.986032","doi":"10.1145/985921.986032","authors":["Jacek Gwizdka"],"year":2004,"abstract":"Examines individual differences in using email to manage future tasks and events. The study identifies contrasting task-management styles and provides evidence that users differ substantially in how they retain, organize, and act on task-related messages.","venue":"CHI 2004 Extended Abstracts"}
{"title":"The Nature of Requests and Commitments in Email Messages","url":"https://researchers.mq.edu.au/en/publications/the-nature-of-requests-and-commitments-in-email-messages/","authors":["Andrew Lampert","Robert Dale","Cécile Paris"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Develops precise utterance-level definitions for requests and commitments in workplace email and applies them to Enron messages. The work is directly relevant to distinguishing responsibility-bearing requests and promises from generic importance or likely user actions.","venue":"AAAI Workshop"}
{"title":"Can requests-for-action and commitments-to-act be reliably identified in email messages?","authors":["Andrew Lampert","Cécile Paris","Robert Dale"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Investigates whether requests for action and commitments to act can be annotated reliably in workplace email. It provides direct evidence that obligation-related speech acts can be treated as distinct annotation targets rather than inferred from response probability or generic importance.","venue":"Australasian Document Computing Symposium"}
{"title":"Requests and Commitments in Email are More Complex Than You Think: Eight Reasons to be Cautious","url":"https://aclanthology.org/U08-1009/","authors":["Andrew Lampert","Robert Dale","Cécile Paris"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Analyzes difficult cases in identifying requests and commitments in workplace email and documents several sources of ambiguity. It offers annotation and modeling guidance for obligation-oriented email processing.","venue":"Australasian Language Technology Association Workshop"}
{"title":"What Did I Ask You to Do, by When, and for Whom?: Passion and Compassion in Request Management","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2998181.2998251","doi":"10.1145/2998181.2998251","authors":["Michael J. Muller","Casey Dugan","Michael Brenndoerfer","Megan Monroe","Werner Geyer"],"year":2017,"published":"2017-02-25","abstract":"Studies workplace request management when requests are scattered across communication channels. It reports difficulties with aggregation, prioritization, context, deadlines, and accountability and derives design implications for tools that maintain actionable requests.","venue":"CSCW 2017"}
{"title":"EPIC: A multi-tiered approach to enterprise email prioritization","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2166966.2167000","doi":"10.1145/2166966.2167000","authors":["Jie Lu","Zhen Wen","Shimei Pan","Jennifer Lai"],"year":2012,"published":"2012-04-26","abstract":"Presents a personalized enterprise-email priority classifier using message content, topic and user models, organizational information, and interaction history. It combines global and user-specific models and evaluates personalized email-priority prediction, but does not independently establish response obligations.","venue":"IUI 2012"}
{"title":"The Learning Behind Gmail Priority Inbox","url":"https://research.google/pubs/the-learning-behind-gmail-priority-inbox/","year":2010,"abstract":"Describes the machine-learning system behind Gmail Priority Inbox, including per-user models that are updated frequently from user behavior. The system is relevant to adaptive personalized ranking, while its targets concern user actions rather than independently labeled workplace obligations.","venue":"NIPS Workshop on Learning on Cores, Clusters and Clouds"}
{"title":"Annotation and Classification of an Email Importance Corpus","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P15-2107/","doi":"10.3115/v1/P15-2107","authors":["Fan Zhang","Kui Xu"],"year":2015,"abstract":"Introduces a manually annotated corpus for email importance and evaluates automatic classification from message text. It provides direct evidence for email-importance modeling but treats importance as a generic target rather than separating obligations from softer user preference.","venue":"ACL-IJCNLP 2015"}
{"title":"Smart To-Do: Automatic Generation of To-Do Items from Emails","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.06282","arxiv_id":"2005.06282","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.acl-main.767","authors":["Sudipto Mukherjee","Subhabrata Mukherjee","Marcello Hasegawa","Ahmed Hassan Awadallah","Ryen White"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Introduces automatic generation of concise to-do items from emails in which a sender has committed to an action. The task provides a mechanism for turning commitment-bearing email content into explicit work items, although it does not evaluate longitudinal work-item state transitions.","venue":"ACL 2020"}
{"title":"Attention-Sensitive Alerting","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.6707","arxiv_id":"1301.6707","authors":["Eric J. Horvitz","Andy Jacobs","David Hovel"],"year":1999,"abstract":"Develops utility-directed alerting that balances the expected cost of delaying information against the cost of interrupting a user. Email criticality is a central application, making the work relevant to urgency- and delay-aware prioritization without equating alert utility with response obligation.","venue":"Conference on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence"}
{"title":"A novel approach to update summarization using evolutionary manifold-ranking and spectral clustering","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2011.08.084","doi":"10.1016/j.eswa.2011.08.084","authors":["Ruifang He","Bing Qin","Ting Liu"],"year":2012,"abstract":"Addresses update summarization over dynamically evolving topic document collections, selecting salient information that is novel relative to material the user is assumed to have already read. It is useful transfer evidence for repeated-cycle novelty control but does not preserve unchanged open obligations by design.","venue":"Expert Systems with Applications"}
{"title":"Mining Sequential Update Summarization with Hierarchical Text Analysis","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/2016/1340973","doi":"10.1155/2016/1340973","authors":["Chunyun Zhang","Zhongwei Si","Zhanyu Ma"],"year":2016,"published":"2016-02-17","abstract":"Studies sequential update summarization in which successive outputs should contain relevant, novel, timely, and concise information. Its evaluation includes gain, latency, and comprehensiveness measures, providing transfer evidence for longitudinal information selection and omission-sensitive evaluation.","venue":"Mobile Information Systems"}
{"title":"Timeline Generation through Evolutionary Trans-Temporal Summarization","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D11-1040/","authors":["Rui Yan","Liang Kong","Congrui Huang","Xiaojun Wan","Xiaoming Li","Yan Zhang"],"year":2011,"abstract":"Generates timelines from time-stamped document collections using summaries that are related across dates. The method models temporal evolution and cross-date content selection, but does not explicitly represent persistent open, resolved, revoked, or superseded work-item states.","venue":"EMNLP 2011"}
{"title":"Evolutionary timeline summarization: A balanced optimization framework via iterative substitution","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2009916.2010016","doi":"10.1145/2009916.2010016","authors":["Rui Yan","Xiaojun Wan","Jahna Otterbacher","Liang Kong","Xiaoming Li","Yan Zhang"],"year":2011,"abstract":"Constructs an evolutionary sequence of dated summaries while balancing relevance, coverage, coherence, and cross-date diversity. It is transfer evidence for reducing temporal redundancy while retaining coverage, rather than direct evidence for workplace work-item persistence.","venue":"SIGIR 2011"}
{"title":"Exploiting relevance, coverage, and novelty for query-focused multi-document summarization","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2013.02.015","doi":"10.1016/j.knosys.2013.02.015","authors":["Wenjuan Luo","Fuzhen Zhuang","Qing He","Zhongzhi Shi"],"year":2013,"abstract":"Presents a query-focused multi-document summarization framework that jointly models relevance to the information need, content coverage, and novelty. It provides transfer mechanisms for balancing omission and repetition but is a static summarization setting rather than a repeated workplace briefing cycle.","venue":"Knowledge-Based Systems"}
{"title":"Beyond SumBasic: Task-focused summarization with sentence simplification and lexical expansion","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipm.2007.01.023","doi":"10.1016/j.ipm.2007.01.023","authors":["Lucy Vanderwende","Hisami Suzuki","Chris Brockett","Ani Nenkova"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Develops task-focused multi-document summarization intended to answer a specified information need, combining content selection with sentence simplification and lexical expansion. It supplies foundational transfer evidence for requirement-directed briefing selection but has no longitudinal accepted-view state.","venue":"Information Processing & Management"}
{"title":"Agent Newsroom: Efficient Chronological Report Generation via Dynamic Multi-Agent Collaboration","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1149/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2026.acl-long.1149","authors":["Zhenhua Wang","Chunlei Wang","Yue Geng","Bang Wang"],"year":2026,"abstract":"Generates chronological reports from evolving document streams using dynamically coordinated agents for coverage and diversity, cross-review to reduce redundancy, duplicate adjudication, and adaptive control. It provides recent transfer evidence for repeated-cycle report generation under evolving input, but not explicit workplace obligation states.","venue":"ACL 2026"}
{"title":"QMSum: A New Benchmark for Query-based Multi-domain Meeting Summarization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.05938","arxiv_id":"2104.05938","doi":"10.18653/v1/2021.naacl-main.472","authors":["Ming Zhong","Da Yin","Tao Yu","Ahmad Zaidi","Mutethia Mutuma","Rahul Jha","Ahmed Hassan Awadallah","Asli Celikyilmaz","Yang Liu","Xipeng Qiu","Dragomir Radev"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Introduces query-based summarization of long meetings, including queries concerning decisions and tasks, and evaluates locate-then-summarize approaches. It is relevant to decision- and action-focused content selection but does not by itself establish cross-conversation incremental work-item tracking.","venue":"NAACL 2021"}
{"title":"Adaptive User Modeling with Long and Short-Term Preferences for Personalized Recommendation","url":"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2019/585","doi":"10.24963/ijcai.2019/585","authors":["Zeping Yu","Jianxun Lian","Ahmad Mahmoody","Gongshen Liu","Xing Xie"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Models both long-term and short-term user preferences and adaptively combines them using time- and content-aware mechanisms. It is transfer evidence for personalization that changes with user behavior rather than remaining a fixed profile.","venue":"IJCAI 2019"}
{"title":"Learning Multi-Objective Rewards and User Utility Function in Contextual Bandits for Personalized Ranking","url":"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2019/532","doi":"10.24963/ijcai.2019/532","authors":["Nirandika Wanigasekara","Yuxuan Liang","Siong Thye Goh","Ye Liu","Joseph Jay Williams","David S. Rosenblum"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Uses contextual bandits to learn multiple ranking objectives together with user-specific utility weights. It is transfer evidence for adaptive multi-objective personalization, but its utility optimization does not independently distinguish mandatory workplace obligations from soft preferences.","venue":"IJCAI 2019"}
{"title":"Scaling up Ranking under Constraints for Live Recommendations by Replacing Optimization with Prediction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.07088","arxiv_id":"2202.07088","authors":["Yegor Tkachenko","Wassim Dhaouadi","Kamel Jedidi"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Studies real-time ranking subject to explicit constraints and approximates an optimization-based constrained-ranking solution with a predictive model. The work is transferable to architectures that enforce hard briefing requirements separately from utility-oriented ranking.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"Lexicographically-ordered constraint satisfaction problems","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10601-009-9069-0","doi":"10.1007/s10601-009-9069-0","authors":["Eugene C. Freuder","Robert Heffernan","Richard J. Wallace","Nic Wilson"],"year":2010,"abstract":"Formalizes constraint problems in which feasibility constraints and preferences are represented separately and preferences can be ordered lexicographically. It supplies a transferable formal mechanism for obligation-before-preference decision rules.","venue":"Constraints"}
{"title":"Fuzzy user-interest drift detection based recommender systems","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/FUZZ-IEEE.2016.7737835","doi":"10.1109/FUZZ-IEEE.2016.7737835","year":2016,"abstract":"Studies recommender systems that detect changes in user interests and adapt recommendations when those interests drift. The work provides transfer evidence for longitudinal personalization under preference change rather than fixed-profile ranking.","venue":"IEEE International Conference on Fuzzy Systems"}
{"title":"Collaborative filtering with temporal dynamics","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1557019.1557072","doi":"10.1145/1557019.1557072","authors":["Yehuda Koren"],"year":2009,"abstract":"Models systematic changes in user preferences and item perceptions over time within collaborative filtering. It is foundational transfer evidence that personalization models can explicitly represent temporal preference drift.","venue":"KDD 2009"}
{"title":"A survey on concept drift adaptation","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2523813","doi":"10.1145/2523813","authors":["João Gama","Indrė Žliobaitė","Albert Bifet","Mykola Pechenizkiy","Abdelhamid Bouchachia"],"year":2014,"published":"2014-03-01","abstract":"Surveys learning under concept drift, including adaptive algorithms, drift-handling strategies, and evaluation methodology for changing data streams. It provides transfer foundations for longitudinally adapting user-priority models when behavior changes.","venue":"ACM Computing Surveys"}
{"title":"Experimenting with prequential variations for data stream learning evaluation","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/coin.12208","doi":"10.1111/coin.12208","authors":["Juan I. González Hidalgo","Bruno I. F. Maciel","Roberto S. M. Barros"],"year":2019,"published":"2019-04-23","abstract":"Compares prequential evaluation variants for data-stream learning, including sliding-window and fading-factor approaches under concept drift. It supplies transfer methodology for evaluating adaptation chronologically without future-data leakage.","venue":"Computational Intelligence"}
{"title":"Time to Split: Exploring Data Splitting Strategies for Offline Evaluation of Sequential Recommenders","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3748164","doi":"10.1145/3705328.3748164","authors":["Danil Gusak","Anna Volodkevich","Anton Klenitskiy","Alexey Vasilev","Evgeny Frolov"],"year":2025,"abstract":"Compares data-splitting strategies for sequential recommender evaluation and shows that split choice can materially change measured performance and model ordering. It highlights temporal leakage risks and motivates global temporal splits when the goal is future-period behavior.","venue":"ACM RecSys 2025"}
{"title":"Optimal use of reminders: Metacognition, effort, and cognitive offloading","url":"https://doi.org/10.1037/xge0000652","doi":"10.1037/xge0000652","authors":["Sam J. Gilbert","Arabella Bird","Jason M. Carpenter","Stephen M. Fleming","Chhavi Sachdeva","Pei-Chun Tsai"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Examines how people choose whether to set reminders when reminders improve prospective-memory performance but impose a cost. The experiments characterize reminder use as a trade-off involving expected remembering, effort, and metacognitive confidence.","venue":"Journal of Experimental Psychology: General"}
{"title":"Confidence guides spontaneous cognitive offloading","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41235-019-0195-y","doi":"10.1186/s41235-019-0195-y","authors":["Annika Boldt","Sam J. Gilbert"],"year":2019,"published":"2019-12-02","abstract":"Shows that people spontaneously use external reminders more when confidence in unaided memory is lower, even after accounting for objective memory ability. Reminder use improves task performance, providing evidence that subjective forgetting risk influences offloading decisions.","venue":"Cognitive Research: Principles and Implications"}
{"title":"Excessive use of reminders: Metacognition and effort-minimisation in cognitive offloading","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.concog.2020.103024","doi":"10.1016/j.concog.2020.103024","authors":["Chhavi Sachdeva","Sam J. Gilbert"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Investigates why people sometimes rely on reminders more than would maximize task reward. The findings implicate both metacognitive underconfidence and effort minimization, making the study relevant to models of reminder value rather than reminder timing alone.","venue":"Consciousness and Cognition"}
{"title":"Framing cognitive offloading in terms of gains or losses: achieving a more optimal use of reminders","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41235-022-00416-3","doi":"10.1186/s41235-022-00416-3","authors":["Lea Fröscher","Ann-Kathrin Friedrich","Max Berentelg","Curtis Widmer","Sam J. Gilbert","Frank Papenmeier"],"year":2022,"published":"2022-07-16","abstract":"Tests whether framing the consequences of reminder use as gains or losses changes reminder-setting behavior. It provides experimental evidence that reminder-selection policies can be shifted toward more optimal cognitive offloading.","venue":"Cognitive Research: Principles and Implications"}
{"title":"Metacognition guides intention offloading and fulfillment of real-world plans","url":"https://doi.org/10.1037/xap0000515","doi":"10.1037/xap0000515","authors":["Ava E. Scott","Sam J. Gilbert"],"year":2024,"published":"2024-07-18","abstract":"Studies reminders for real-world upcoming plans and finds that reminder setting is related to metacognitive confidence and perceived importance. Reminder use is also associated with greater subsequent fulfillment of intentions, directly connecting offloading decisions with real-world prospective action.","venue":"Journal of Experimental Psychology: Applied"}
{"title":"Influence of the physical effort of reminder-setting on strategic offloading of delayed intentions","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/17470218231199977","doi":"10.1177/17470218231199977","authors":["Gavin Chiu","Sam J. Gilbert"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Uses preregistered experiments to test how the physical cost of setting reminders affects strategic offloading of delayed intentions, including interactions with memory demands. It contributes evidence about the cost-benefit determinants of whether an outstanding intention is externally supported.","venue":"Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology"}
{"title":"Strategic reminder setting for time-based intentions: Influence of metacognition, delay length, and cue visibility","url":"https://doi.org/10.3758/s13421-025-01708-x","doi":"10.3758/s13421-025-01708-x","authors":["Pei-Chun Tsai","Sam J. Gilbert"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-04-16","abstract":"Examines reminder selection for time-based intentions as a function of metacognitive beliefs, delay length, and cue visibility. Reminder use increases under conditions associated with greater prospective-memory difficulty, providing evidence about forgetting-risk signals relevant to content selection.","venue":"Memory & Cognition"}
{"title":"How Do Viewers Synthesize Conflicting Information from Data Visualizations?","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.03828","arxiv_id":"2208.03828","doi":"10.1109/TVCG.2022.3209467","authors":["Prateek Mantri","Hariharan Subramonyam","Audrey L. Michal","Cindy Xiong"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Uses multiple experiments to study how viewers combine sequential pieces of conflicting visual evidence. It characterizes how evidence is weighted when later information disagrees with earlier information and derives implications for presenting evolving or disputed evidence to decision makers.","venue":"IEEE Transactions on Visualization and Computer Graphics"}
{"title":"Effects of visualizing uncertainty on decision-making in a target identification scenario","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cag.2014.02.006","doi":"10.1016/j.cag.2014.02.006","authors":["Maria Riveiro","Tove Helldin","Göran Falkman","Mikael Lebram"],"year":2014,"abstract":"Evaluates decision making under time pressure with and without explicit uncertainty visualization in a target-identification and prioritization setting. The study measures how uncertainty displays affect operators' decisions, priorities, and behavior rather than only subjective visualization preference.","venue":"Computers & Graphics"}
{"title":"Uncertainty Visualization and Decision Making: Does Visualizing Uncertain Information Change Decisions?","url":"https://www.sci.utah.edu/~kpotter/Library/Papers/deitrick%3A2007%3AUVDM/index.html","authors":["Stephanie A. Deitrick"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Reports a human-subject experiment examining whether alternative visual representations of uncertainty change decisions and confidence. The effects depend on the task, making the study relevant transfer evidence for experimentally comparing uncertainty presentations in decision-support briefings.","venue":"International Cartographic Conference"}
{"title":"The Influence of Uncertainty Visualization on Decision Making: An Empirical Evaluation","url":"https://www.sci.utah.edu/~kpotter/Library/Papers/deitrick%3A2006%3AIUDM/index.html","authors":["Stephanie Deitrick","Robert Edsall"],"year":2006,"abstract":"Empirically evaluates how uncertainty visualization influences users' decisions and confidence. It provides transfer evidence that presentation choices for uncertain information can be tested against decision outcomes rather than assessed solely for readability.","venue":"Progress in Spatial Data Handling"}
{"title":"Conflicting information integration for decision support","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0167-9236(86)90003-5","doi":"10.1016/0167-9236(86)90003-5","year":1986,"abstract":"Examines methods for integrating conflicting information from multiple sources for decision-support purposes and proposes an information-theoretic treatment. It is mechanism-level evidence for handling disputed multi-source state rather than an experiment on workplace briefing presentation.","venue":"Decision Support Systems"}
